A||||22||fogglethorpe||Name the party||||1||
Z||000000||fogglethorpe||08-05-2001||02:45 PM||||<BR>Democrats have no ideas.<BR>Republicans have ideas, but no spines.<BR>Libertarians have ideas and spines, but no candidates.<BR>Independents have ideas, spines, and candidates, but no strength of unity.<P>So...what to do?<P>I have decided to start my own political party, and I am currently tossing around a few name ideas. Here they are:<P>-The stop boinking interns and lying about it party.<BR>-The speak some damned English instead of Orwellian doublespeak party<BR>-The serve your time, get the hell out of congress, get a real job, and live under the legislation you helped write party<BR>-The read and learn the Constitution, then never try to pass laws that violate it party<BR>-The serve your constituents who elected you instead of having a bloated ego and an appetite for power party<P>Unfortunately, none of these fit on a bumper sticker...<P>------------------<BR>I don't think so;therefore, I'm probably not.||||reg||1
Z||000001||btoth||08-05-2001||03:01 PM||||How about "The common sense party"?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>The bumper stickers could say "Got common sense?"  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||maryjane||08-05-2001||03:04 PM||||"Then what to do"<BR>Wait for dictatorship then we'll really have something to bit** about, but won't be allowed to.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Songman||08-05-2001||03:27 PM||||Personally, it sounds like some good ideas to me. Too bad people won't stay that way after they get to Washington..<P>I like what Jesse Ventura said about the fact that we didn't have 'career politicians' in the early days. Guys came and did their good deeds for their country and then went back to their farms and factories. I know that nowadays people have to know more to handle the 'world situation' but I still know there has to be a better way.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
Z||000004||EdsB52||08-05-2001||04:27 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by fogglethorpe:<BR><B><BR>Democrats have no ideas.<BR>Republicans have ideas, but no spines.<BR>Libertarians have ideas and spines, but no candidates.<BR>Independents have ideas, spines, and candidates, but no strength of unity.<P>So...what to do?<P>I have decided to start my own political party, and I am currently tossing around a few name ideas. Here they are:<P>-The stop boinking interns and lying about it party.<BR>-The speak some damned English instead of Orwellian doublespeak party<BR>-The serve your time, get the hell out of congress, get a real job, and live under the legislation you helped write party<BR>-The read and learn the Constitution, then never try to pass laws that violate it party<BR>-The serve your constituents who elected you instead of having a bloated ego and an appetite for power party<P>Unfortunately, none of these fit on a bumper sticker...<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>"Democrats have no ideas."<P>Sure they do, what do you call socialized medicine? It's an idea, maybe just not a popular one. Education reform for all citizens, not just private school attendees.<P>"Republicans have ideas, but no spines."<P>Sure they have spines, what do you call governmentally funded faith-based charities? Whacking the Ergonomics Bill, giving away the federal surplus that was accrued during the Clinton years, removing the ABA's ability to rate federal judges before appointment, and much more are all indicators of having spines, but just no concern for the lower class (you and me).<P>"Libertarians have ideas and spines, but no candidates."<P>Many Libertarian ideas are pretty screwy.<P>"Independents have ideas, spines, and candidates, but no strength of unity."<P>Ross Perot didn't have good ideas? He received over 19% of the populous vote, however no electoral votes. He involuntarily helped vote out disaster Sr., so he can't be all bad!<P>"The speak some damned English instead of Orwellian doublespeak party"<P>Referring to Bush Jr with his wacky invented words?! Or to Quale (sp) with his potatoe?<P>"The read and learn the Constitution, then never try to pass laws that violate it party"<P>I hope you're not referring to that old document with 27 Amendments. The US Constitution is found in volumes of precedent, not the original writing of the Bill of Rights or subsequent Amendments. I agree with you that it's all hosed up, so what is your solution? I don't like a representative form of government. It's better than a monarchy, but not much. I would like a form of government that would allow the people to vote on issues as they emerge, not an a**holes as they indulge. I love to vote propositions when they come up with the election ballot. I pay far more attention to them than the candidates.||||reg||1
Z||000005||fogglethorpe||08-05-2001||08:14 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by EdsB52:<P><BR>I hope you're not referring to that old document with 27 Amendments. The US Constitution is found in volumes of precedent, not the original writing of the Bill of Rights or subsequent Amendments. I agree with you that it's all hosed up, so what is your solution? I don't like a representative form of government. It's better than a monarchy, but not much. I would like a form of government that would allow the people to vote on issues as they emerge, not an a**holes as they indulge. I love to vote propositions when they come up with the election ballot. I pay far more attention to them than the candidates.[/B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>In my opinion, one of the worst forms of government is Democracy.The framers of our Constitution were just as afraid of mob rule by the ignorant masses as they were of the king. That's why we began as a Republic, which is rule by laws. Ever hear the phrase, "Democracy is when two foxes and one lamb vote on what to have for lunch."?<BR>I do agree that the Constitution of the United States has been watered down by activist judges, and ignorance of elected officials.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000006||maryjane||08-05-2001||08:29 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by fogglethorpe:<BR>In my opinion, one of the worst forms of government is Democracy......<P>[/B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Guess that depends what you're comparing it to. How many places have you lived or visited that weren't a democracy? <BR>There is always the possibility one fox isn't hungry for mutton. What if there was just one wolf and the lamb? Course, I sometimes wonder if even we have a democracy.<BR>Remember last Nov?||||reg||1
Z||000007||EdsB52||08-05-2001||08:41 PM||||B][/QUOTE]<BR>In my opinion, one of the worst forms of government is Democracy.The framers of our Constitution were just as afraid of mob rule by the ignorant masses as they were of the king. That's why we began as a Republic, which is rule by laws. Ever hear the phrase, "Democracy is when two foxes and one lamb vote on what to have for lunch."?<BR>I do agree that the Constitution of the United States has been watered down by activist judges, and ignorance of elected officials.<P>[/B][/QUOTE]<P>"In my opinion, one of the worst forms of government is Democracy.The framers of our Constitution were just as afraid of mob rule by the ignorant masses as they were of the king."<P>I agree with that assessment, well put.<P>"Democracy is when two foxes and one lamb vote on what to have for lunch."<P>No, I haven't heard that one, but I do like it!<P>"I do agree that the Constitution of the United States has been watered down by activist judges, and ignorance of elected officials."<P>Actually I wasn't saying that at all. I love the idea of the Supreme Court, both State and U.S.  AS far as activist judges, do you mean judges with bias? Bias is an inherent component of all humans and always will be. Don't forget, America was founded by revolutionary people, as well as by murderers and thieves. As for elected officials, let me say this; If and when I become an attorney I will be forever grateful for the existence of politicians!||||reg||1
Z||000008||EdsB52||08-05-2001||08:49 PM||||[/B][/QUOTE]<BR>Guess that depends what you're comparing it to. How many places have you lived or visited that weren't a democracy? <BR>There is always the possibility one fox isn't hungry for mutton. What if there was just one wolf and the lamb? Course, I sometimes wonder if even we have a democracy.<BR>Remember last Nov?[/B][/QUOTE]<P>"Course, I sometimes wonder if even we have a democracy. Remember last Nov?"<P>Awesome point! How can a democracy include an electoral college? Not to mention inherent bias with the Governor of the State in question being the brother of one of those running and the Secretary of State in the State in question being a member of the election committee. What a joke.<P>As far as which places are not democracies; England, Arabic nations, and many others are monarchies. Much of the world does not subscribe to democracy.||||reg||1
Z||000009||maryjane||08-05-2001||09:11 PM||||Now Ed, even you will have to admit that just as some countries are democracies in name only(even Cuba has elections), that England is nowhere near a real monarchy.<BR>(What's happened to you Don? never argue religion, politics, or footbal!)||||reg||1
Z||000010||Raydar||08-05-2001||10:07 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by EdsB52:<BR><B> Many Libertarian ideas are pretty screwy.<P>Ross Perot didn't have good ideas? He received over 19% of the populous vote, however no electoral votes. He involuntarily helped vote out disaster Sr., so he can't be all bad!<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I don't think the idea of repealing all the laws concerning victimless crimes is screwy at all. The less government intervention, the better. There's a lot of stuff that just isn't anybody's business but my own.<P>Ross Perot was crazy as a bedbug. He'd have nuked somebody inside of his first year in office. <BR>As for the election? <BR>He's the only reason the Commander-In-Briefs got elected.<BR>Clinton couldn't have done it on his own merits. He <I>sure</I> didn't win a majority of the popular vote.<P><P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000011||EdsB52||08-06-2001||01:29 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by maryjane:<BR><B>Now Ed, even you will have to admit that just as some countries are democracies in name only(even Cuba has elections), that England is nowhere near a real monarchy.<BR>(What's happened to you Don? never argue religion, politics, or footbal!)</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>True. Most governments are quasi-democracies. And in England isn't the Parliament elected??? I just don't like forms of government that are totally representative.||||reg||1
Z||000012||EdsB52||08-06-2001||01:45 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Raydar:<BR><B> I don't think the idea of repealing all the laws concerning victimless crimes is screwy at all. The less government intervention, the better. There's a lot of stuff that just isn't anybody's business but my own.<P>Ross Perot was crazy as a bedbug. He'd have nuked somebody inside of his first year in office. <BR>As for the election? <BR>He's the only reason the Commander-In-Briefs got elected.<BR>Clinton couldn't have done it on his own merits. He <I>sure</I> didn't win a majority of the popular vote.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>"I don't think the idea of repealing all the laws concerning victimless crimes is screwy at all."<P>I agree. Crimes like prostitution should not be crimes at all. Did someone say moral subjugation? I must be hearing things. With your position I certainly hope you have never voted Republican or subscribed to mass religion.<P>"The less government intervention, the better."<P>Yes and no. What do we do with people that are socially or physically disabled? They just won't disappear on their own. Actually they will if we let them suffer and starve. That's where my critique of the Libertarian party resides, in social welfare; we need it and the Libertarians don't make exception for them to my knowledge (correct me if I'm wrong). With smaller government, we are less able to help those in need.<P>"There's a lot of stuff that just isn't anybody's business but my own.<P>Agree whole-heartedly. Privacy (the one word the founding fathers forgot to write) is at the crux of liberty. I'm a big 4th guy and our Reagan appointed Supreme Court is smashing the crap out of it.<P>"Ross Perot was crazy as a bedbug. He'd have nuked somebody inside of his first year in office."<P>He was wacky but harmless. Bush Sr. was far more hazardous. You still didn't address the fact that an independent received 19% of the populous vote.<P>"As for the election? <BR>He's the only reason the Commander-In-Briefs got elected.<BR>Clinton couldn't have done it on his own merits. He <I>sure</I> didn't win a majority of the popular vote."<P>I voted for Perot, hence indirectly voted for Clinton. Clinton wasn't voted in, Bush was voted out. Face it Bush Sr. was a disaster. I did like his dealings in the Gulf War though. Didn't Clinton win more populous votes than Bush?||||reg||1
Z||000013||Raydar||08-06-2001||10:32 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by EdsB52:<BR><B>Crimes like prostitution should not be crimes at all. Did someone say moral subjugation? I must be hearing things. With your position I certainly hope you have never voted Republican or subscribed to mass religion.<P>What do we do with people that are socially or physically disabled? They just won't disappear on their own. Actually they will if we let them suffer and starve. That's where my critique of the Libertarian party resides, in social welfare; we need it and the Libertarians don't make exception for them to my knowledge (correct me if I'm wrong). With smaller government, we are less able to help those in need.<P>Privacy (the one word the founding fathers forgot to write) is at the crux of liberty. I'm a big 4th guy and our Reagan appointed Supreme Court is smashing the crap out of it.<P>(Ross Perot)was wacky but harmless. Bush Sr. was far more hazardous. You still didn't address the fact that an independent received 19% of the populous vote.<P>I voted for Perot, hence indirectly voted for Clinton. Clinton wasn't voted in, Bush was voted out. Face it Bush Sr. was a disaster. I did like his dealings in the Gulf War though. Didn't Clinton win more populous votes than Bush?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><I>"With your position I certainly hope you have never voted Republican or subscribed to mass religion."</I><P>Actually, I have voted Republican on many occasions. Mostly because they seem to be the lesser of the evils. I always vote Libertarian if it looks like that vote will not help the Democrats. <BR>I don't belong to an organized religion. I do pray on a regular basis. <BR>I went to catholic school through the 8th grade, and while it was a bit extreme, it surely didn't do me any harm, and quite probably may have done me some good.  <BR>I *do* have a problem with other people's religion being forced down my throat, though.<P><I>"What do we do with people that are socially or physically disabled? They just won't disappear on their own. Actually they will if we let them suffer and starve."</I> <P>I believe that as the tax burden increases, charitable contributions decrease. I believe that if the tax rate went down significantly, charitable contributions would increase. These contributions (to churches or private organizations) would help to take care of the infirm. In many cases, a spouse (the lower paid one) works only to take care of the tax burden. If taxes were significantly lower, that person could stay at home and take care of the kids. Many more families would <I>have</I> kids. This would also help with the care of the aged folks. Families used to be able to  take care of their elders, before the elders outnumbered the offspring.<P><I>"Privacy (the one word the founding fathers forgot to write) is at the crux of liberty. I'm a big 4th guy and our Reagan appointed Supreme Court is smashing the crap out of it."</I><P>Agreed. But the Clintonistas weren't any better.<BR>And the war on "consumable substances" is the primary reason/excuse for this. The Libertarian philosophy would do away with this. <P><I>"(Ross Perot)was wacky but harmless. Bush Sr. was far more hazardous. You still didn't address the fact that an independent received 19% of the populous vote."</I><P>I disagree. When I think of Perot, the term "loose cannon" comes to mind. Although congress may have been able to stop him from doing anything <I>really</I> stupid, there's always the power of the Executive Order &lt;shudder&gt;. <BR>How was G.B. Sr. more hazardous? <BR>A lot of people blame him for a poor economy, but the economy was recovering <BR>by the time he left office, due to things that were implemented during his administration.<BR>And a lot of people have tried to blame the current economic slump on W. <BR>Baloney. The economy started to slide months before the election. W inherited it.<BR>Perot received 19% of the vote, because many people liked and believed him. On the face of things, he seemed like a good idea. I considered voting for him, myself. He just appeared to be out of control. I think that Stockdale(?), his running mate, damaged Perot's credibility immensely. <P><I>"I voted for Perot, hence indirectly voted for Clinton. Clinton wasn't voted in, Bush was voted out. Face it Bush Sr. was a disaster. I did like his dealings in the Gulf War though. Didn't Clinton win more populous votes than Bush?"</I><P>Bush wasn't really voted out. Perot just split the Republican vote. Clinton <I>did</I> win more popular votes than Bush, but he still didn't win a majority. There should exist the possibility of a runoff, in our presidential elections. <BR>How was Bush Sr. a disaster? If he'd been in office for another year or so, the economic reforms that were implemented during his term would have had a chance to work while he was still there, and he'd have come out smelling like a rose.<P>Of course, many of my responses (especially the one regarding elderly care) are theories.<BR>We probably will never get a chance to see who's correct.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><BR><P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000014||fogglethorpe||08-06-2001||07:55 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by maryjane:<BR><B> [QUOTE]Originally posted by fogglethorpe:<BR>In my opinion, one of the worst forms of government is Democracy......<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Guess that depends what you're comparing it to. How many places have you lived or visited that weren't a democracy? <BR>There is always the possibility one fox isn't hungry for mutton. What if there was just one wolf and the lamb? Course, I sometimes wonder if even we have a democracy.<BR>Remember last Nov?[/B][/QUOTE]<P>Actually, we don't have a democracy, and never did. We have a republic. There are elements of democracy at work, but we are not a pure democracy, and for good reason, as I have stated above.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000015||fogglethorpe||08-06-2001||07:58 PM||||Oh, and as far as sour grapes over the last election, read Article II of the U.S. Constitution. The president is not, and never was, elected by popular vote.<BR>The electoral college actually makes the process into 50 separate elections.||||reg||1
Z||000016||btoth||08-06-2001||08:05 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by fogglethorpe:<BR><B> We have a republic.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I learned that in school.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  (Yeah, I sound stupid for saying that.)<P>On a side note:  don't you think that with today's technology, that they could actually get a more acurate vote from the population instead of using the electoral college method which is obviously flawed?<P>||||reg||1
Z||000017||EdsB52||08-06-2001||10:12 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by btoth:<BR><B> I learned that in school.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  (Yeah, I sound stupid for saying that.)<P>On a side note:  don't you think that with today's technology, that they could actually get a more acurate vote from the population instead of using the electoral college method which is obviously flawed?<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Actually you get a more accurate election with the Electoral College. I hate to say that but it's true. You also have a better chance of winning if you have a large State's Secretary of State in you pocket and the Governor as your brother. If you're a redneck and close the polls early to disallow the minority vote you won't hurt your chances either. In a representative government, the clowns in suits are representatives. If you lived in North Dakota whenever it became a State, or Montana or Alaska when they became States, your comparatively few votes wouldn't represent your State's population very well and in effect not be counted. With electors appointed to States' based upon population you have a voice commensurate to your population.<P>I wish we had a hybrid electoral/populous system where we weight the votes if it's close enough.<P>It's not a lack of technology when obvious coercion exists in a given jurisdiction, the polls close early, people that would probably give an adverse vote are turned away, ballot cards are assembled in an ambiguous format, and the US Supreme Court are such big pussies they won't touch the issue with a 10 foot pole and kick it back to the State court.||||reg||1
Z||000018||DRH||08-06-2001||10:45 PM||||Constitutionally we don't even have to vote for President. The power to designate electors for the electorial college is given to the states. Each state legislature could pick it's own electors if it chose to set it up that way. That's one reason the USSC kept kicking it back to FL. There are constitutional provisions to handle the whole thing. If it weren't for the media hype I believe the USSC would have refused to hear it and let it be decided in Congress (guess which way it would have gone there anyway).<P>The number of electors for each state is based on it's representation in Congress. This give each state some voice while still giving the more populous states more voice. The states are the ones that decide whether their electors are winner take all or apportioned by the percentage of the state vote.<P>We actually had a worse case of president by appointment in the 1800s. Several states (FL was in the middle of it then too) sent two slates of electors. A panel was appointed to decide which were valid. They, of course, voted straight down party lines and Rutherford B Hayes was "appointed" President. Of course we didn't have CNN and company pushing the panic button 24/7 back then...<P>||||reg||1
Z||000019||Cheever2||08-07-2001||12:36 AM||||The real power is in the hands of the international bankers, whose ultimate goal is one-world government (to serve their own interests more efficiently).  Everything else is just a sideshow distraction.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000020||EdsB52||08-07-2001||05:47 AM||||[QUOTE]Originally posted by Raydar:<BR>[B] [i<BR>"Actually, I have voted Republican on many occasions. Mostly because they seem to be the lesser of the evils. I always vote Libertarian if it looks like that vote will not help the Democrats."<P>Proving my theory that Libertarians are nothing more than disgruntled Republicans. If they (Republicans) are the lesser of the evils, you must not care at all about 4th Amendment issues or the US Constitution in general.<P>"I went to catholic school through the 8th grade, and while it was a bit extreme, it surely didn't do me any harm, and quite probably may have done me some good.  <BR>I *do* have a problem with other people's religion being forced down my throat, though."<P>Glad to see the moral coercion/brainwashing didn't affect you. Nothing wrong with practicing religion, public or private, but when it even starts to cross into the mainstream and you find yourself going to great measures to avoid it, it has extended beyond the reaches of 1st Amendment guarantees. <P>"I believe that as the tax burden increases, charitable contributions decrease. I believe that if the tax rate went down significantly, charitable contributions would increase. These contributions (to churches or private organizations) would help to take care of the infirm. In many cases, a spouse (the lower paid one) works only to take care of the tax burden. If taxes were significantly lower, that person could stay at home and take care of the kids. Many more families would <I>have</I> kids. This would also help with the care of the aged folks. Families used to be able to  take care of their elders, before the elders outnumbered the offspring."<P>Come on, that tax burden conjecture is ridiculous, and this where many of the critiques of Libertarian philosophies get born and flourish. Does a disabled person or the elderly wait for the church to receive contributions? How do you adjust for an ill economy where church donations (10%) are down? Sounds idealistic to ignore them and hope the church bears the burden, unless you become paralyzed.<P>"Agreed. But the Clintonistas weren't any better.<BR>And the war on "consumable substances" is the primary reason/excuse for this. The Libertarian philosophy would do away with this."<P>Really, do you know whom Clinton appointed to the US Supreme Court? Do you know whom disaster senior appointed? Do you know whom Reagan appointed? After that, what decisions have these people levied? How have these decisions rewritten the Constitution, especially the 4th? Now we can talk about the 4th Amendment and the entire Constitution.<P>"I disagree. When I think of Perot, the term "loose cannon" comes to mind. Although congress may have been able to stop him from doing anything <I>really</I> stupid, there's always the power of the Executive Order &lt;shudder&gt;."<P>Pure conjecture, but he was a businessman not a warmonger. He probably would have left the military to handle their business while he tried to fix the economy. He had no interest in the military, nor did he have big dreams of changing it. In all his televised ramblings I never once saw him refer to the military, although I'm sure he did touch on it.<P>"How was G.B. Sr. more hazardous? <BR>A lot of people blame him for a poor economy, but the economy was recovering <BR>by the time he left office, due to things that were implemented during his administration."<P>He signed the largest tax increase in history. Read my lips......oh never mind. He had his NRA ticket pulled due to his signing of the Assault Rifle Bill. That's all I can think of without research, but that's plenty. In most cases it's fallacious to credit/blame a President for sole destruction/resurrection of the economy. You subscribed to that fallacy in your argument.<P>"And a lot of people have tried to blame the current economic slump on W. <BR>Baloney."<P>I like that; W. Baloney, it fits! Again, I have not credited Clinton nor blamed Reagan or Bush Jr or Sr for their dealings with the economy, you have. However, I do wonder what impact, positive or negative, the 'giving back' of the budget surplus will have on the future economy. It seems like both a desperate grasp and a move toward political popularity for W. Baloney to disperse the surplus.<P>"Perot received 19% of the vote, because many people liked and believed him. On the face of things, he seemed like a good idea. I considered voting for him, myself. He just appeared to be out of control. I think that Stockdale(?), his running mate, damaged Perot's credibility immensely."<P>What political campaign is designed to make people hate you and disbelieve in you? The truth is people had enough of Reaganomics (trickle-down), Bush Sr, and the Republican Party so they were thrown the other way(s). Perot seemed like a break from bureaucratic politics, which was what we needed. As a Libertarian (which is essentially an independent) I would think your party would immolate some of the ideas of another non-mainstream party that had more success than any other non-mainstream party has probably ever had.<P>"Bush wasn't really voted out. Perot just split the Republican vote. Clinton <I>did</I> win more popular votes than Bush, but he still didn't win a majority. There should exist the possibility of a runoff, in our presidential elections. <BR>How was Bush Sr. a disaster? If he'd been in office for another year or so, the economic reforms that were implemented during his term would have had a chance to work while he was still there, and he'd have come out smelling like a rose."<P>That's what I was saying; bush was voted out by virtue of a split Republican vote. That's amazing, a disgruntled Republican turned Libertarian complaining about an election that wasn't close! There were no brothers as Governors, no Secretary of State under desks, and no ballot improprieties.<P>"Of course, many of my responses (especially the one regarding elderly care) are theories.<BR>We probably will never get a chance to see who's correct."<P>No, because they're too extreme for even the nut-jobs we have in office now.||||reg||1
Z||000021||EdsB52||08-07-2001||05:49 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by fogglethorpe:<BR><B>Oh, and as far as sour grapes over the last election, read Article II of the U.S. Constitution. The president is not, and never was, elected by popular vote.<BR>The electoral college actually makes the process into 50 separate elections.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>50 seperate elections for the purpose of equal representation.||||reg||1
Z||000022||EdsB52||08-07-2001||06:05 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by DRH:<BR><B><BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>"Constitutionally we don't even have to vote for President. The power to designate electors for the electorial college is given to the states. Each state legislature could pick it's own electors if it chose to set it up that way."<P>Yep.<P>"That's one reason the USSC kept kicking it back to FL. There are constitutional provisions to handle the whole thing."<P>Nope. The USSC wanted nothing to do with it but felt obligated. Unlike Jed and the whore, the USSC felt they had to handle the matter with kid gloves considering most of them were appointed by disaster senior and the senile one (uh, mommy). Constitutional provisions......um, do you know what the role of the USSC is? <P>"If it weren't for the media hype I believe the USSC would have refused to hear it and let it be decided in Congress (guess which way it would have gone there anyway)."<P>The media distorts everything. I think the USSC had to at least review it, that's why they certified it. <P>"The number of electors for each state is based on it's representation in Congress. This give each state some voice while still giving the more populous states more voice."<P>Another way of saying the number of electors is based upon that State's population, as I wrote.<P>"The states are the ones that decide whether their electors are winner take all or apportioned by the percentage of the state vote."<P>Right, but when was the last time a State apportioned their electoral votes?||||reg||1
